WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.550
Regularization,

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:04.190
it's just another way to say

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.270
desensitization.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:07.990
Let's check it out with ridge

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:12.470
regression, StatQuest.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.830
Hello, I'm Josh Starmer and welcome to

00:00:14.840 --> 00:00:17.150
StatQuest. Today, we're going to do part

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.630
one of a series of video on

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.030
regularization techniques. In this

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.510
video, we're going to cover ridge

00:00:22.520 --> 00:00:24.310
regression and it's going to be clearly

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.390
explained.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:28.750
Note, this StatQuest assumes you

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:30.710
understand the concepts of bias and

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.670
variance in the context of machine

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:35.430
learning. If not, check out machine

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.430
learning fundamentals, bias and

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.470
variance.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.590
It also assumes that you are familiar

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:44.310
with linear models. If not, check out

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:46.590
the following StatQuests. The links are

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:49.510
in the description below.

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:52.070
Lastly, if you're not already familiar

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.510
with the concept of cross-validation,

00:00:54.520 --> 00:00:55.630
check out the StatQuest on

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:57.470
cross-validation.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.430
In this StatQuest, we will one, look at

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.230
a simple example that shows the main

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.990
ideas behind ridge regression,

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.710
two, go into details about how ridge

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.910
regression works,

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.710
three, show how ridge regression works

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:16.350
in a variety of situations,

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.030
and four, lastly, we'll talk about how

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:20.870
ridge regression can solve the

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.790
unsolvable.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.230
Bam!

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.190
Let's start by collecting weight and

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.350
size measurements from a bunch of mice.

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.070
Since these data look relatively linear,

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.030
we will use linear regression, aka least

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:38.350
squares, to model the relationship

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:41.310
between weight and size.

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.430
So, we'll fit a line to the data using

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.070
least squares.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.230
In other words, we find the line that

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.550
results in the minimum sum of squared

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.390
residuals.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.070
Ultimately, we end up with this equation

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:56.910
for the line.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:59.870
The line has two parameters,

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:02.350
a Y axis intercept

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:04.430
and a slope.

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:06.750
We can plug in a value for weight, for

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:09.630
example, 2.5,

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:11.870
and do the math,

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.750
and get a value for size.

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:18.430
Together, the value for weight, 2.5, and

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:21.949
the value for size, 2.8, give us a point

00:02:21.959 --> 00:02:24.270
on the line.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.310
When we have a lot of measurements, we

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.110
can be fairly confident that the least

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.350
squares line accurately reflects the

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.910
relationship between size and weight.

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.350
But what if we only have two

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.510
measurements?

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:41.510
We fit a new line with least squares.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.830
Since the new line overlaps the two data

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.030
points, the minimum sum of squared

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:49.510
residuals equals zero.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:51.990
Ultimately, we end up with this equation

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.230
for the new line.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.910
Note, here are the original data and the

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:59.910
original line for comparison.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.350
Let's call the two red dots the training

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.070
data, and the remaining green dots the

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.110
testing data.

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.870
The sum of the squared residuals for

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.270
just the two red points, the training

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:14.350
data, is small. In this case, it is

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.030
zero.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:17.990
But the sum of the squared residuals for

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.830
the green points, the testing data, is

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:22.590
large.

00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:24.790
And that means that the new line has

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:26.910
high variance.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.430
In machine learning lingo, we'd say that

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.190
the new line is overfit to the training

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:33.509
data.

00:03:33.519 --> 00:03:35.509
Now, let's go back to just the training

00:03:35.519 --> 00:03:36.949
data.

00:03:36.959 --> 00:03:39.390
We just saw that least squares results

00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:41.830
in a line that is overfit and has high

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:43.830
variance.

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:46.630
The main idea behind ridge regression is

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:48.910
to find a new line that doesn't fit the

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:51.590
training data as well.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.910
In other words, we introduce a small

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:56.310
amount of bias into how the new line is

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:58.430
fit to the data.

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:00.230
But in return for that small amount of

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.710
bias, we get a significant drop in

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:04.590
variance.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.550
In other words, by starting with a

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:09.270
slightly worse fit, ridge regression can

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:12.670
provide better long-term predictions.

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:15.510
Bam!

00:04:15.520 --> 00:04:17.670
Now let's dive into the nitty-gritty and

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:20.789
learn how ridge regression works.

00:04:20.799 --> 00:04:24.030
Let's go back to just the training data.

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:26.270
When least squares determines values for

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.830
the parameters in this equation,

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:30.790
it minimizes

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:34.430
the sum of the squared residuals.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.950
In contrast, when ridge regression

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:38.870
determines the values for the parameters

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.630
in this equation,

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:42.470
it minimizes

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:45.110
the sum of the squared residuals

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:50.550
plus lambda times the slope squared.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:53.470
Note, I usually try to avoid using Greek

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:55.870
characters as much as possible, but if

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:57.310
you are ever going to do ridge

00:04:57.320 --> 00:04:59.590
regression in practice, you have to know

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:03.350
that this term is called lambda.

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:05.830
This part of the equation adds a penalty

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:08.950
to the traditional least squares method.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.350
And lambda determines how severe that

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:13.630
penalty is.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:16.110
To get a better idea of what's going on,

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:18.750
let's plug in some numbers.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:20.670
Let's start by plugging in the numbers

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:22.710
that correspond to the least squares

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:24.510
fit.

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:26.510
The sum of the squared residuals for the

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:29.310
least squares fit is zero because the

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:32.670
line overlaps the data points.

00:05:32.680 --> 00:05:36.470
And the slope is 1.3.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:38.870
We'll talk more about lambda later, but

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:42.670
for now, let lambda equal one.

00:05:42.680 --> 00:05:48.670
Altogether, we have 0 + 1 * 1.3 squared.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:51.470
And when we do the math, we get

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:54.710
1.69.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.550
Now, let's see what happens when we plug

00:05:56.560 --> 00:05:58.270
in numbers for the ridge regression

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:00.030
line.

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:03.470
The sum of the squared residuals is

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:07.030
0.3 squared for this residual

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:11.790
plus 0.1 squared for this residual.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:15.470
The slope is 0.8.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:17.950
And just like before, we'll let lambda

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.390
equal 1.

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.030
Altogether, we have 0.3 squared plus 0.1

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:29.750
squared plus 1 * 0.8 squared.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:32.830
And when we do the math, we get

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:36.270
0.74.

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:38.870
For the least squares line, the sum of

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:40.870
squared residuals plus the ridge

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:45.150
regression penalty is 1.69.

00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:47.590
For the ridge regression line, the sum

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:49.590
of squared residuals plus the ridge

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:53.950
regression penalty is 0.74.

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:56.550
Thus, if we wanted to minimize the sum

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:58.830
of the squared residuals plus the ridge

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:01.230
regression penalty, we would choose the

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:03.590
ridge regression line over the least

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:05.670
squares line.

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:07.710
Without the small amount of bias that

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:10.430
the penalty creates, the least squares

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:13.830
fit has a large amount of variance.

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:16.870
In contrast, the ridge regression line,

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:18.950
which has a small amount of bias due to

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:22.950
the penalty, has less variance.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:25.950
Now, before we talk about lambda, let's

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:28.030
talk a little bit more about the effect

00:07:28.040 --> 00:07:29.990
that the ridge regression penalty has on

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.070
how the line is fit to the data.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:35.550
To keep things simple, imagine we only

00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:38.190
have one line.

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:40.350
This line suggests that for every one

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:42.750
unit increase in weight,

00:07:42.760 --> 00:07:44.750
there is a one unit increase in

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.590
predicted size.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:50.590
If the slope of the line is steeper,

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:52.470
then for every one unit increase in

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:53.830
weight,

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:56.190
the prediction for size increases by

00:07:56.200 --> 00:07:58.590
over two units.

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:00.630
In other words, when the slope of the

00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:02.830
line is steep, then the prediction for

00:08:02.840 --> 00:08:05.270
size is very sensitive to relatively

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:08.390
small changes in weight.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:10.670
When the slope is small,

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:12.550
then for every one unit increase in

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:13.830
weight,

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:15.950
the prediction for size barely

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:18.030
increases.

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:20.270
In other words, when the slope of the

00:08:20.280 --> 00:08:23.030
line is small, then predictions for size

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.230
are much less sensitive to changes in

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:27.390
weight.

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:29.630
Now, let's go back to the least squares

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:31.630
and ridge regression lines fit to the

00:08:31.640 --> 00:08:33.750
two data points.

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:36.230
The ridge regression penalty resulted in

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:39.510
a line that has a smaller slope,

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:41.310
which means that predictions made with

00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:43.510
the ridge regression line are less

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:45.630
sensitive to weight than the least

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:47.630
squares line.

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:49.790
Bam!

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:51.670
Now, let's go back to the equation that

00:08:51.680 --> 00:08:54.430
ridge regression tries to minimize,

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:57.230
and talk about lambda.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:08:59.750
Lambda can be any value from zero to

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:01.870
positive infinity.

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:04.310
When lambda equals zero, then the ridge

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:07.750
regression penalty is also zero.

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:09.590
And that means that the ridge regression

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:12.030
line will only minimize the sum of

00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:14.710
squared residuals.

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:16.750
And the ridge regression line will be

00:09:16.760 --> 00:09:19.110
the same as the least squares line,

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:20.910
because they are both minimizing the

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:23.950
exact same thing.

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:25.590
Now, let's see what happens as we

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:28.470
increase the value for lambda.

00:09:28.480 --> 00:09:30.990
In the example we just looked at, we set

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:33.070
lambda equals one, and the ridge

00:09:33.080 --> 00:09:35.350
regression line ended up with a smaller

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:38.630
slope than the least squares line.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:41.270
When we set lambda equals two, the slope

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:43.630
gets even smaller.

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:45.990
And when we set lambda equals three, the

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:48.790
slope is even smaller.

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:51.270
And the larger we make lambda, the slope

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:55.310
gets asymptotically close to zero.

00:09:55.320 --> 00:09:57.390
So, the larger lambda gets, our

00:09:57.400 --> 00:09:59.990
prediction for size become less and less

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:03.030
sensitive to weight.

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:05.070
So, how do we decide what value to give

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:07.310
lambda?

00:10:07.320 --> 00:10:09.790
We just try a bunch of values for lambda

00:10:09.800 --> 00:10:11.830
and use cross-validation.

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:14.630
Typically, 10-fold cross-validation to

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:16.510
determine which one results in the

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:19.150
lowest variance.

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:21.830
Double bam!

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:24.310
In the previous example, we showed how

00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:26.230
ridge regression would work when we want

00:10:26.240 --> 00:10:28.550
to predict size, which is a continuous

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:29.910
variable,

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:32.430
using weight, which is also a continuous

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:34.590
variable.

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:37.150
However, ridge regression also works

00:10:37.160 --> 00:10:39.350
when we use a discrete variable, like

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:41.950
normal diet versus high-fat diet, to

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:44.750
predict size.

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:46.990
In this case, the data might look like

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:48.430
this.

00:10:48.440 --> 00:10:50.630
And the least squares fitted equation

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:52.710
might look like this.

00:10:52.720 --> 00:10:54.430
Where 1.5,

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:56.550
the equivalent of a Y intercept,

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:58.470
corresponds to the average size of the

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:01.030
mice on the normal diet.

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:02.870
And 0.7,

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:05.270
the equivalent of a slope, corresponds

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:06.910
to the difference between the average

00:11:06.920 --> 00:11:09.310
size for the mice on the normal diet

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:11.190
compared to the mice on the high-fat

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:13.070
diet.

00:11:13.080 --> 00:11:16.190
Note, from here on out, we'll refer to

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:20.150
this distance as diet difference.

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:23.150
High-fat diet is either zero for mice on

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:25.270
the normal diet

00:11:25.280 --> 00:11:29.670
or one for mice on the high-fat diet.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:32.390
In In words, this term alone predicts

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:36.270
the size of mice on the normal diet.

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:38.510
And the sum of these two terms is the

00:11:38.520 --> 00:11:40.590
prediction for the size of mice on the

00:11:40.600 --> 00:11:43.230
high-fat diet.

00:11:43.240 --> 00:11:45.350
For the mice on the normal diet, the

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:47.430
residuals are the distances between the

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:51.110
mice and the normal diet mean.

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:53.550
And for mice on the high-fat diet, the

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:55.510
residuals are the distances between the

00:11:55.520 --> 00:11:59.230
mice and the high-fat diet mean.

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:01.230
When least squares determines the values

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:03.710
for the parameters in this equation,

00:12:03.720 --> 00:12:05.950
it minimizes the sum of the squared

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:07.910
residuals.

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:10.350
In other words, these distances between

00:12:10.360 --> 00:12:13.670
the data and the means are minimized.

00:12:13.680 --> 00:12:15.750
When ridge regression determines values

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:18.190
for the parameters in this equation,

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:20.350
it minimizes the sum of the squared

00:12:20.360 --> 00:12:21.910
residuals

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:24.270
plus lambda times diet difference

00:12:24.280 --> 00:12:26.310
squared.

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:29.070
Remember, diet difference simply refers

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:30.990
to the distance between the mice on the

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:33.430
normal diet and the mice on the high-fat

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:35.430
diet.

00:12:35.440 --> 00:12:37.750
When lambda equals zero,

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:41.510
this whole term ends up being zero.

00:12:41.520 --> 00:12:43.470
And we get the same equation that we got

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:46.190
with least squares.

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:48.670
But when lambda gets large,

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:50.430
the only way to minimize the whole

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:52.190
equation

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:55.950
is to shrink diet distance down.

00:12:55.960 --> 00:12:58.910
In other words, as lambda gets larger,

00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:01.030
our prediction for the size of mice on

00:13:01.040 --> 00:13:03.910
the high-fat diet becomes less sensitive

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:05.510
to the difference between the normal

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:09.070
diet and the high-fat diet.

00:13:09.080 --> 00:13:11.150
And remember, the whole point of doing

00:13:11.160 --> 00:13:13.270
ridge regression is because small sample

00:13:13.280 --> 00:13:15.390
sizes like these

00:13:15.400 --> 00:13:17.910
can lead to poor least squares estimates

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:19.990
that result in terrible machine learning

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:22.110
predictions.

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:24.670
Bam!

00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:26.990
Ridge regression can also be applied to

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:29.350
logistic regression.

00:13:29.360 --> 00:13:31.830
In this example, we are using weight to

00:13:31.840 --> 00:13:35.110
predict if a mouse is obese or not.

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:37.110
This is the equation for this logistic

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:38.950
regression.

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:40.830
And ridge regression would shrink the

00:13:40.840 --> 00:13:42.830
estimate for the slope, making our

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:44.590
prediction about whether or not a mouse

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:48.630
is obese less sensitive to weight.

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:50.670
Note, when applied to logistic

00:13:50.680 --> 00:13:53.270
regression, ridge regression optimizes

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:55.510
the sum of the likelihoods instead of

00:13:55.520 --> 00:13:57.870
the squared residuals because logistic

00:13:57.880 --> 00:13:59.870
regression is solved using maximum

00:13:59.880 --> 00:14:02.190
likelihood.

00:14:02.200 --> 00:14:04.830
So far, we've seen simple examples of

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:06.750
how ridge regression helps reduce

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:09.030
variance by shrinking parameters and

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:10.950
making our predictions less sensitive to

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:12.950
them.

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:14.590
But we can apply ridge regression to

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:17.630
complicated models as well.

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:19.670
In this model, we've combined the weight

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:22.510
measurement data from the first example

00:14:22.520 --> 00:14:24.190
with the two diets from the second

00:14:24.200 --> 00:14:26.310
example.

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:28.430
Combining these two data sets gives us

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:30.750
this equation.

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:32.710
And ridge regression tries to minimize

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:34.310
this.

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:35.950
Now the ridge regression penalty

00:14:35.960 --> 00:14:38.270
contains the parameters for the slope

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:41.070
and the difference between diets.

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:43.630
In general, the ridge regression penalty

00:14:43.640 --> 00:14:46.310
contains all of the parameters except

00:14:46.320 --> 00:14:49.070
for the Y intercept.

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:53.750
If we had a big, huge, crazy equation

00:14:53.760 --> 00:14:56.310
with terms for astrological sign, the

00:14:56.320 --> 00:14:59.670
air speed of a swallow, and other stuff,

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:01.710
then the ridge regression penalty would

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:04.390
have all those parameters squared except

00:15:04.400 --> 00:15:07.190
for the Y intercept.

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:09.190
Every parameter except for the Y

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:12.910
intercept is scaled by the measurements.

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:14.990
And that's why the Y intercept is not

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:16.550
included in the ridge regression

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:18.590
penalty.

00:15:18.600 --> 00:15:21.390
Double bam.

00:15:21.400 --> 00:15:23.630
Okay. Now the next thing we're going to

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:25.430
talk about is going to sound totally

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:28.030
random, but trust me, it will lead to

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:29.470
the coolest thing about ridge

00:15:29.480 --> 00:15:30.870
regression.

00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:34.910
It's so cool, it's almost like magic.

00:15:34.920 --> 00:15:36.750
We all know that this is the equation

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:38.510
for a line.

00:15:38.520 --> 00:15:40.470
And in order for least squares to solve

00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:42.550
for the parameters, the Y intercept and

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:43.990
slope,

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:47.190
we need at least two data points.

00:15:47.200 --> 00:15:49.110
These data points result in these

00:15:49.120 --> 00:15:50.830
parameters

00:15:50.840 --> 00:15:53.870
and this specific line.

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:56.470
If we only had one data point,

00:15:56.480 --> 00:15:58.150
then we wouldn't be able to solve for

00:15:58.160 --> 00:15:59.870
these parameters

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:01.510
because there would be no way to tell if

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:02.990
this line

00:16:03.000 --> 00:16:06.550
is better than this line or this line

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:08.470
or any old line that goes through the

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:10.590
one data point.

00:16:10.600 --> 00:16:13.190
All of these lines have zero residuals

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:15.390
and thus all minimize the sum of the

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:18.030
squared residuals.

00:16:18.040 --> 00:16:20.270
It's not until we have two data points

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:22.310
that it becomes clear that this is the

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:25.270
least squares solution.

00:16:25.280 --> 00:16:27.470
Now, let's look at an equation that has

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:30.310
three parameters to estimate.

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:33.150
We need to estimate a Y intercept,

00:16:33.160 --> 00:16:34.829
a slope that reflects how weight

00:16:34.839 --> 00:16:37.790
contributes to the prediction of size,

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:39.710
and a slope that reflects how age

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:43.230
contributes to the prediction of size.

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:44.790
When we have three parameters to

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:47.230
estimate, then just two data points

00:16:47.240 --> 00:16:49.829
isn't going to cut it.

00:16:49.839 --> 00:16:52.150
That's because in three dimensions,

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:53.910
which is what we get when we add another

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:56.630
axis to our graph for age, we have to

00:16:56.640 --> 00:16:58.829
fit a plane to the data instead of just

00:16:58.839 --> 00:17:00.310
a line.

00:17:00.320 --> 00:17:02.390
And with only two data points, there's

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:04.550
no reason why this plane fits the data

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:06.069
any better than

00:17:06.079 --> 00:17:07.470
this plane

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:09.030
or this plane.

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:10.710
But as soon as we have three data

00:17:10.720 --> 00:17:11.990
points,

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:15.189
we can solve for these parameters.

00:17:15.199 --> 00:17:16.750
If we have an equation with four

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:18.189
parameters,

00:17:18.199 --> 00:17:20.470
then least squares needs at least four

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:22.470
data points to estimate all four

00:17:22.480 --> 00:17:24.350
parameters.

00:17:24.360 --> 00:17:27.069
And if we have an equation with 10,001

00:17:27.079 --> 00:17:28.510
parameters,

00:17:28.520 --> 00:17:31.350
then we need at least 10,001 data points

00:17:31.360 --> 00:17:34.590
to estimate all of the parameters.

00:17:34.600 --> 00:17:37.430
An equation with 10,001 parameters might

00:17:37.440 --> 00:17:39.710
sound bonkers, but it's more common than

00:17:39.720 --> 00:17:42.030
you might expect.

00:17:42.040 --> 00:17:44.150
For example, we might use gene

00:17:44.160 --> 00:17:46.070
expression measurements from 10,000

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:48.950
genes to predict size.

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:50.790
And that would mean we would need gene

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:53.110
expression measurements from 10,001

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:54.910
mice.

00:17:54.920 --> 00:17:56.430
Unfortunately,

00:17:56.440 --> 00:17:58.150
collecting gene expression measurements

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:01.270
from 10,001 mice is crazy expensive and

00:18:01.280 --> 00:18:03.910
time-consuming right now.

00:18:03.920 --> 00:18:06.950
In practice, a huge data set might have

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:10.230
measurements from 500 mice.

00:18:10.240 --> 00:18:12.630
So, what do we do if we have an equation

00:18:12.640 --> 00:18:16.030
with 10,001 parameters and only 500 data

00:18:16.040 --> 00:18:17.630
points?

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:20.750
We use ridge regression.

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:22.950
It turns out that by adding the ridge

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:25.310
regression penalty, we can solve for all

00:18:25.320 --> 00:18:28.550
10,001 parameters with only 500 or even

00:18:28.560 --> 00:18:30.950
fewer samples.

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:32.670
One way to think about how ridge

00:18:32.680 --> 00:18:34.710
regression can solve for parameters when

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:37.070
there isn't enough data is to go back to

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:40.550
our original size versus weight example.

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:43.070
Only this time, there's only one data

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:45.710
point in the training set.

00:18:45.720 --> 00:18:47.870
Least squares can't find a single

00:18:47.880 --> 00:18:50.270
optimal solution since any line that

00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:52.190
goes through the dot will minimize the

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:55.270
sum of the squared residuals.

00:18:55.280 --> 00:18:57.710
But ridge regression can find a solution

00:18:57.720 --> 00:18:59.710
with cross-validation and the ridge

00:18:59.720 --> 00:19:02.030
regression penalty that favors smaller

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:04.230
parameter values.

00:19:04.240 --> 00:19:06.670
Since this StatQuest is already super

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:08.590
long, we'll save a more thorough

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:10.350
discussion of how this works for a

00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:12.870
future StatQuest.

00:19:12.880 --> 00:19:15.310
Triple bam!

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:17.030
In summary,

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:19.070
when the sample sizes are relatively

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:21.710
small, then ridge regression can improve

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:24.470
predictions made from new data, i.e.,

00:19:24.480 --> 00:19:26.550
reduce variance by making the

00:19:26.560 --> 00:19:28.390
predictions less sensitive to the

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:30.550
training data.

00:19:30.560 --> 00:19:32.110
This is done by adding the ridge

00:19:32.120 --> 00:19:33.950
regression penalty to the thing that

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:36.550
must be minimized.

00:19:36.560 --> 00:19:38.990
The ridge regression penalty itself is

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:41.790
lambda times the sum of all squared

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:45.510
parameters except for the Y intercept.

00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:47.350
And lambda is determined using

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:49.910
cross-validation.

00:19:49.920 --> 00:19:52.390
Lastly, even when there isn't enough

00:19:52.400 --> 00:19:54.430
data to find the least squares parameter

00:19:54.440 --> 00:19:56.790
estimates, ridge regression can still

00:19:56.800 --> 00:19:59.430
find a solution using cross-validation

00:19:59.440 --> 00:20:02.150
and the ridge regression penalty.

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:04.230
Hooray! We've made it to the end of

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:06.750
another exciting StatQuest. If you like

00:20:06.760 --> 00:20:08.670
this StatQuest and want to see more,

00:20:08.680 --> 00:20:10.510
please subscribe. And if you want to

00:20:10.520 --> 00:20:13.390
support StatQuest, well, consider buying

00:20:13.400 --> 00:20:15.750
one or two of my original songs. All

00:20:15.760 --> 00:20:20.560
right. Until next time, quest on.

